  "title": "Dinner-to-sleep interval and next-morning fasting glucose, 60 people",
  "alt": "Scatter of 60 people: fasting glucose against the hours between dinner and sleep, with a gentle downward tilt from left to right.",
  "longDescription": "Each of the 60 dots is one person. The horizontal position is the interval between that person's dinner and going to sleep, from 0.8 to 6.5 hours; the vertical position is the next-morning fasting glucose, from 100 to 181 mg/dL. The cloud is wide at every interval, so the interval does not determine anybody's glucose. Across the cloud, the dots drift downwards from left to right: shorter intervals, meaning later dinners, sit a little higher. The 30 people with an interval of three hours or less average 146.5 mg/dL and the 30 with an interval of more than three hours average 137.5 mg/dL, a difference of 9.0 mg/dL. Several people with the shortest intervals have lower glucose than several people with the longest, which is why the tilt is described as mild rather than clear. The 60 vertical positions are the same 60 values shown in the histogram earlier in this lesson.",
